Day and Night Sky by DimensionAlmios (Dimension Almios)
https://godotshaders.com/shader/day-and-night-sky-with-sun-moon-stars-and-clouds/

The licence statement that covers this shader appears in two places.

1. In the shader's own header comment:
   "Day and Night Sky (free) by Dimension Almios, MIT licence."

2. In the licence notice at the foot of the shader's page on Godot Shaders (the
   page above):
   "The shader code and all code snippets in this post are under MIT license and
   can be used freely. Images and videos, and assets depicted in those, do not
   fall under this license."
   (the notice links the MIT licence, https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT, and
   the site's terms, https://godotshaders.com/license/)

This port contains the shader code only. No images, screenshots or other assets
from the page are used.

The shader's hash() helper follows the form of David Hoskins' "Hash without
Sine" (the same constants, 0.1031 and 33.33), which is also published under the
MIT licence, Copyright (c)2014 David Hoskins. The licence text below applies to
that part as well.

Ported to Metal Shading Language for MacOS Live Wallpaper; see the note at the
top of main.metal for the full list of changes. In short:
  - The Godot wrapper (shader_type, fragment(), UV, FRAGCOORD, TIME, COLOR, node
    modulate) is removed and the exposed uniforms are constants at their
    defaults.
  - The time-of-day input `hour` is replaced by the real sky: the sun's height
    comes from lwSunElevation and its east-west position from lwSunAzimuth, so
    the sky follows the real day at the user's place. The moon's phase comes from
    lwMoon, with its lit edge facing the sun. The dawn/dusk palette choice is a
    smooth blend on the sun's side of the sky instead of hour < 12.
  - Clouds drift at 0.4 of the original speed (cloud_speed 0.006).
  - Metal syntax only otherwise: reversed-edge smoothstep() is a helper, and a
    few names are changed. The cloud loop and the moon code are skipped where
    their result is provably nothing.

